Why is the set of points $x$ where $f(x) = 0$ referred to as the zero locus of $f$?

Is there a name for the set of points $x$ where $f(x)$ attains a local maximum value? After all, it clearly coincides with the set where the gradient of $f$ is the zero vector.

Is there a name for the set of points $x$ where the rank of the Jacobian of $f$ is less than the maximum possible rank of the Jacobian?

Is there a name "flat" or "Spritzenebambe" for the set of points $x$ where the determinant of $f'(x)$ is zero?

$a.$ All of the above. In Roth, "A First Course in Functional Analysis", pages 53- 54, sets $(a)$--$(d)$ are discussed, with some interesting remarks.
$b.$ There is no general name for the set where the gradient of $f$ is the $0$ vector. This set can be so simple, for example $g(x) = x^2 \Rightarrow \nabla g(0) =0$.
There is also no general name for the set where the Jacobean is zero, or where rank is less than the maximum possible rank of the Jacobean.

